  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" Reply A is the text: \"Here is a draft:\\n```json\\n{\\\"a\\\": 1} {\\\"b\\\": 2}\\n```\\nFinal answer:\\n{\\\"c\\\": 3}\". Reply B is the text: \"Final answer:\\n{\\\"c\\\": 3}\". For Reply A, the rule requires the answer to be the last object inside the code fence, which is {\"b\": 2}, because a fence holds one. For Reply B, there is no fence, so the rule requires the last top-level object, which is {\"c\": 3}. The check, however, returns {\"c\": 3} for both: in Reply A the fenced pool contains the whole fence content \"{\\\"a\\\": 1} {\\\"b\\\": 2}\", which is not a single JSON object and is passed over, and the top-level scan then finds the later {\"c\": 3} as the last top-level object. The conjecture would be false if recover_json_object(Reply A) returned {\"b\": 2} or any value different from recover_json_object(Reply B), or if the two replies were not told apart as the rule requires."
